      "passed": false,
      "reasons": [
        "The definition says higher codability means people can “describe it more consistently and effectively in language,” but the packet supports consistency (e.g., “more consistency”) and a “modest advantage” in naming; it does not explicitly support the “effectively” component as a distinct criterion.",
        "The packet frames codability in terms of how well experts/novices can linguistically express smells and flavors, but it does not directly define codability as a general “ease” construct across experiences beyond describing it as “more easily linguistically expressed” (the definition’s wording is partially supported but the effectiveness claim is not)."
